{
 "dataset": "pancreas-human",
 "protocol": "'Known cell-state dictionary' scenario for the population-OT prototype (NOT strict: an upper-bound probe).\n\nQuestion: is population OT weak only because a strict NMF cannot know the cell states that appear later (pancreas\nkeeps opening new directions), or because composition change does not carry the per-gene change at all?\nHere the archetypes (CP10k NMF, cell fractions, archetype count laws A) are fitted ONCE on ALL bins, as if the\ncell-state dictionary came from an external atlas. Given the dictionary, the composition pi_b of bin b depends on\nbin b only, so the composition forecasters (persist, lin1, ot0.05, uot0.05t1, sb3) still read pi_0..pi_i only;\n'oracle' uses the dictionary's pi_{i+1}. The rest is popot.py unchanged (mixture shift of the gene's own pooled law,\nobservation model for orig, plug-in for l1 / linear, rolling-origin choice of state x K within each forecaster).\nEverything here leaks the future through the dictionary (A contains late-bin profiles), so read it only as the\nceiling of 'population OT with a perfect state atlas', never as a strict result.\nUsage: python popot_atlas.py <dataset>  ->  popot_atlas_<dataset>.json\n",
